1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:06,567
A hockey player lines up a shot. He swings. Except that isn't a puck.

2
00:00:06,567 --> 00:00:07,521
It's a watch.

3
00:00:07,521 --> 00:00:12,955
It flies across the ice, into the goalie's glove — and it's still running.

4
00:00:12,955 --> 00:00:16,774
That was a real television commercial, back in 1984.

5
00:00:16,774 --> 00:00:19,785
And most people simply didn't believe it.

6
00:00:19,785 --> 00:00:24,044
They assumed the ad was a trick, because of course it was.

7
00:00:24,044 --> 00:00:25,732
No watch survives that.

8
00:00:25,732 --> 00:00:31,166
So a news programme decided to find out for themselves. On air. Same shot.

9
00:00:31,166 --> 00:00:34,985
Same conditions. We'll come back to that, I promise.

10
00:00:34,985 --> 00:00:40,566
Because here's the part that surprises people. That ad was a rescue mission.

11
00:00:40,566 --> 00:00:43,503
The watch in it had already failed once.

12
00:00:43,503 --> 00:00:52,682
It went on sale in Japan a year earlier and sold so poorly that watch shops didn't really want it — and the reason is lovely.

13
00:00:52,682 --> 00:00:57,161
And the man who made it had spent two years breaking watches.

14
00:00:57,161 --> 00:00:59,364
More than two hundred of them.

15
00:00:59,364 --> 00:01:06,120
Up to a third-floor bathroom, open the window, drop it, walk downstairs, pick up the pieces.

16
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:07,368
Then do it again.

17
00:01:07,368 --> 00:01:13,390
What finally solved it was sitting in a park, watching a kid bounce a rubber ball.

18
00:01:13,390 --> 00:01:17,061
So, three questions. Why didn't the shops want it?

19
00:01:17,061 --> 00:01:19,852
What did that bouncing ball teach him?

20
00:01:19,852 --> 00:01:25,726
And how does a watch nobody wanted end up on more than a hundred million wrists?

21
00:01:25,726 --> 00:01:28,149
This is the story of the G-Shock.

22
00:01:28,149 --> 00:01:33,510
And it starts, the way these stories usually do, with something breaking.

23
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:36,112
His name is Kikuo Ibe.

24
00:01:36,112 --> 00:01:44,853
He's a young engineer at Casio, and one morning on his way to work, his watch comes off his wrist and hits the pavement.

25
00:01:44,853 --> 00:01:47,257
It doesn't crack. It comes apart.

26
00:01:47,257 --> 00:01:51,846
Springs, screws, tiny pieces of metal, all over a Tokyo street.

27
00:01:51,846 --> 00:01:54,104
His father gave him that watch.

28
00:01:54,104 --> 00:02:00,004
So Ibe goes back to his desk and writes a proposal for a watch that cannot break.

29
00:02:00,004 --> 00:02:03,500
Inside Casio it gets a name: Project Team Tough.

30
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:07,725
And three targets, which the company called the Triple 10.

31
00:02:07,725 --> 00:02:13,188
Survive a ten-metre drop. Ten bars of water pressure. Ten years of battery.

32
00:02:13,188 --> 00:02:17,631
In 1981, that sounds impossible. A watch is a delicate thing.

33
00:02:17,631 --> 00:02:22,511
You take it off to play sports. You take it off to wash the dishes.

34
00:02:22,511 --> 00:02:27,901
But here's the thing people forget. Casio wasn't an old Swiss watch house.

35
00:02:27,901 --> 00:02:32,344
Casio made calculators, and came into watches through quartz.

36
00:02:32,344 --> 00:02:36,642
Which means Ibe wasn't trying to protect springs and gears.

37
00:02:36,642 --> 00:02:42,105
A quartz module is tiny. Solid state. Ibe had a heart small enough to hide.

38
00:02:42,105 --> 00:02:44,800
He just had no idea where to hide it.

39
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:49,054
So he started dropping them out of that window.

40
00:02:49,054 --> 00:02:54,455
Over a hundred prototypes in the first year alone. Thicker case. Harder resin.

41
00:02:54,455 --> 00:02:58,886
More padding, more bracing, more armour. Every single one broke.

42
00:02:58,886 --> 00:03:04,009
And they all broke for the same reason — although nobody could see it yet.

43
00:03:04,009 --> 00:03:06,986
Every prototype was built on the same idea.

44
00:03:06,986 --> 00:03:11,556
That to survive a hit, a watch has to be strong enough to take it.

45
00:03:11,556 --> 00:03:14,810
By his own account, Ibe was close to giving up.

46
00:03:15,810 --> 00:03:21,342
Then he sat down in a park. There was a kid nearby, bouncing a rubber ball.

47
00:03:21,342 --> 00:03:25,178
Ibe watched it. Hit the ground. Squash. Spring back.

48
00:03:25,178 --> 00:03:30,857
And the middle of that ball barely moves. The outside takes the whole impact.

49
00:03:30,857 --> 00:03:32,554
The centre just floats.

50
00:03:32,554 --> 00:03:37,127
So he stopped trying to armour the watch, and hung it instead.

51
00:03:37,127 --> 00:03:40,815
In a G-Shock, the module isn't bolted to the case.

52
00:03:40,815 --> 00:03:49,297
It's suspended — held at a few points, cushioned, sitting inside a hollow shell that's allowed to squash around it.

53
00:03:49,297 --> 00:03:54,092
The shock doesn't reach the watch. It gets used up on the way in.

54
00:03:54,092 --> 00:03:58,960
Forty years later, that's still the trick inside every single one.

55
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:05,253
April 1983. The first one goes on sale. The DW-5000C. Square. Black.

56
00:04:05,253 --> 00:04:10,390
Plastic-looking. And in 1983, a proper watch meant steel and thin.

57
00:04:10,390 --> 00:04:15,838
Japan wasn't interested. And here's the answer to that first question.

58
00:04:15,838 --> 00:04:23,778
Some of the resistance came from the shops themselves — and when you hear why, it makes perfect sense.

59
00:04:23,778 --> 00:04:29,226
A watch that never breaks is a watch that never comes back for repair.

60
00:04:29,226 --> 00:04:34,986
Durability wasn't a selling point. For part of the trade, it was bad news.

61
00:04:34,986 --> 00:04:39,423
Ibe said later he was sure the sales would simply dry up.

62
00:04:39,423 --> 00:04:43,470
That's the first time this watch nearly disappeared.

63
00:04:44,470 --> 00:04:47,541
What saved it wasn't Japan. It was American TV.

64
00:04:47,541 --> 00:04:50,088
Which brings us back to the rink. 1984.

65
00:04:50,088 --> 00:04:53,224
Casio runs that commercial in the United States.

66
00:04:53,224 --> 00:04:57,863
The slapshot, the watch as a puck — and nobody believes a second of it.

67
00:04:57,863 --> 00:05:02,762
People assume Casio faked the whole thing, and honestly, why wouldn't they?

68
00:05:02,762 --> 00:05:06,617
It looks exactly like something an ad agency would make up.

69
00:05:06,617 --> 00:05:10,798
So a news programme did what no marketing budget could ever buy.

70
00:05:10,798 --> 00:05:15,045
They ran the test themselves, on air. Same shot. Same conditions.

71
00:05:15,045 --> 00:05:19,160
And the watch kept running. That's the moment everything turns.

72
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:27,980
Because you can build the toughest watch on earth and it's worth nothing — until somebody with no reason to lie shows people it's true.

73
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:36,151
After that, the G-Shock found the people who needed it before it found the people who wanted it.

74
00:05:36,151 --> 00:05:39,363
Police. Firefighters. Rescue crews. Divers.

75
00:05:39,363 --> 00:05:43,472
Anyone whose watch was equipment rather than jewellery.

76
00:05:43,472 --> 00:05:46,460
And Casio did something rather charming.

77
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:52,212
Instead of one tough watch, it started building one for each kind of trouble.

78
00:05:52,212 --> 00:05:57,889
Mud and dust in 1985 — later known as the Mudman. Then divers, pilots, cold.

79
00:05:57,889 --> 00:06:03,865
In 1993 came the Frogman, the first one certified as a proper ISO diver's watch.

80
00:06:03,865 --> 00:06:07,974
And in 1994, the one that defined the era. The DW-6600.

81
00:06:07,974 --> 00:06:13,651
Cheap, light, two hundred metres down. It ended up all over the US military.

82
00:06:13,651 --> 00:06:19,254
Including Navy SEALs, who show up wearing it in the Navy's own photographs.

83
00:06:19,254 --> 00:06:21,196
Which is worth pausing on.

84
00:06:21,196 --> 00:06:26,575
For decades, the watch on those wrists was an expensive mechanical tool.

85
00:06:26,575 --> 00:06:32,700
It was quietly replaced by a plastic one that cost less than a good pair of boots.

86
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:37,531
Then something happened that Ibe never planned for.

87
00:06:37,531 --> 00:06:43,241
In the nineties, people stopped buying G-Shocks for what they could survive.

88
00:06:43,241 --> 00:06:48,575
They started buying them for what they said. Skaters. Surfers. Hip-hop.

89
00:06:48,575 --> 00:06:53,458
That big, chunky case the Japanese shops had turned down in 1983?

90
00:06:53,458 --> 00:06:58,041
It turned out to be exactly the right shape for the nineties.

91
00:06:58,041 --> 00:07:07,807
In 1994 Casio launched Baby-G — the same floating idea, just smaller — and it became one of those things everybody's sister owned.

92
00:07:07,807 --> 00:07:13,441
And this is the part that's easy to miss. The engineering earned the trust.

93
00:07:13,441 --> 00:07:16,672
But it was culture that did the conquering.

94
00:07:16,672 --> 00:07:24,710
The watch became a way of saying something about yourself, whether or not you ever went near a hockey rink.

95
00:07:25,710 --> 00:07:27,262
How big did this get?

96
00:07:27,262 --> 00:07:32,214
Casio says it shipped the hundred millionth G-Shock in August 2017.

97
00:07:32,214 --> 00:07:35,539
They held a ceremony at the Yamagata factory.

98
00:07:35,539 --> 00:07:39,973
That watch got a caseback engraved with one hundred million.

99
00:07:39,973 --> 00:07:50,320
By the fortieth anniversary in 2023, Casio put the total above a hundred and forty million, sold in more than a hundred and forty countries.

100
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:55,419
Those are the company's own numbers. Here's one that isn't marketing.

101
00:07:55,419 --> 00:08:00,888
In 2023, Casio got a three-dimensional trademark on the shape of the case.

102
00:08:00,888 --> 00:08:03,697
Not the name. Not the logo. The shape.

103
00:08:03,697 --> 00:08:08,870
A silhouette you can recognise without anybody telling you what it is.

104
00:08:09,870 --> 00:08:13,138
One last thing. The G-Shock is not indestructible.

105
00:08:13,138 --> 00:08:16,341
That word belongs to the adverts, not to physics.

106
00:08:16,341 --> 00:08:21,179
Hit one hard enough, in the right place, and it breaks like anything else.

107
00:08:21,179 --> 00:08:25,951
So what did Ibe actually invent? Not a stronger watch. A better question.

108
00:08:25,951 --> 00:08:30,984
Everybody before him asked how to make a watch tough enough to survive a hit.

109
00:08:30,984 --> 00:08:34,122
He asked how to stop the hit from ever arriving.

110
00:08:34,122 --> 00:08:40,462
And the answer was to let the important part float — and let everything around it get sacrificed.

111
00:08:40,462 --> 00:08:45,626
Which is a strange kind of monument to a watch that broke on a street in Tokyo.

112
00:08:45,626 --> 00:08:49,810
Not a watch that couldn't be broken. A watch that refused to be.
